<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Dudley, 1st Earl of Leicester</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1532–<a href="/years/1588">1588</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Dudley, 1st Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>, was an English courtier and favourite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nonsuch">Treaty of Nonsuch</a> she sent him with an English army to aid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rebels against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1585, and the States General made him governor-general. His attempts to centralise power and his military failures alienated the province of Holland, and he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1587, after which the States governed as a republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-dudley-1st-earl-of-leicester.jpg" alt="Robert Dudley, 1st Earl of Leicester" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steven van der Meulen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Dudley,_1st_Earl_of_Leicester,_Collection_of_Waddesdon_Manor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rebels offered sovereignty first to Francis, Duke of Anjou, and, after William the Silent was assassinated at Delft in 1584, to Elizabeth I of England, who declined it but sent troops under the <b>Earl of Leicester</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
